#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa2879fd-b388-54d3-91a6-9889b3cc921c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402121} {:answer/id #uuid "7f291e69-4dda-53a6-8a62-f0061587ecc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402122} {:answer/id #uuid "8c43d172-267c-5edc-a00c-eb37c3afc069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402123} {:answer/id #uuid "6160111a-8bd8-53b3-8c8a-0ec9830b724b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402124} {:answer/id #uuid "a82130d9-20ec-5094-9c98-83b539f3238e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c43d172-267c-5edc-a00c-eb37c3afc069" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402123} :answer-field/id #uuid "d370b658-9d56-5718-913a-a06cb231e944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c065292-5916-5335-a2ce-48232ddb8e4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-333856" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5bc609b-dc5b-52e9-9f0e-206f32bb829b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Homogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be54f80-3e00-5c60-8d2c-859b73f1b04a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402127} {:answer/id #uuid "a489112a-2e25-5139-8701-0da71be4eaed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402128} {:answer/id #uuid "f40839a9-1ee4-555c-b0e0-836cd8ef8a5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402129} {:answer/id #uuid "d13024ed-e948-5192-b456-f8fc65a79957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402130} {:answer/id #uuid "2d239718-30d6-534b-aa0f-8cec27c7ffe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d239718-30d6-534b-aa0f-8cec27c7ffe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402131} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbee732a-a84c-5f81-aebe-d7513334919d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "783e954f-0aef-53d9-a6ee-3a8685affb71" :question/math-academy-id "q-334772" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb88ef39-e68a-5400-8035-7c504e8a0052" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Falling Body Model With Quadratic Drag"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32063b22-d699-5f63-a71d-869c73971f55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g-kv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402134} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc50789-5421-50d2-b60e-67a3bd2e10f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402135} {:answer/id #uuid "491ac020-a720-59c1-ba10-c392f69847b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=2g-k{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402136} {:answer/id #uuid "4948e46f-d047-5768-bf2d-e94a4743506f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402137} {:answer/id #uuid "d34ed5ad-0c4e-5b97-a8e7-e19f41dfe4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4948e46f-d047-5768-bf2d-e94a4743506f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402137} :answer-field/id #uuid "8915c372-42a6-5420-8c91-5f069925b152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28461e48-9c29-58e8-b99d-2d64a173813c" :question/math-academy-id "q-335312" :question/problem "A rock of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the rock's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the rock is proportional to the square of the rock's velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, the acceleration due to gravity is $g,$ and the only forces acting on the rock are the weight due to gravity and drag." :question/worked-solution "Recall Newton's second law, which states that the resultant force $F$ acting on a body equals the product of its mass $m$ and acceleration $a:$\n\n$$\nF=ma\n$$\n\nIn our case, two forces are acting on the rock:\n\n- The force due to gravity, given by the weight $w$ of the rock, which equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =mg \\\\  & =2g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The force due to drag (air resistance) $D,$ which is proportional to the square of the velocity of the rock (and negative since it acts in the negative direction):\n\n$$\nD=-k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality. Note that, since drag, in $\\text{N}=\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ is the product of the constant $k$ and squared velocity, in $(\\text{m/s})^{2}={\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{N}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m/s}}^{2}}{{\\text{m}}^{2}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}=\\text{kg/m}.\n$$\n\nSumming the forces, the resultant force acting on the rock is\n\n$$\nF=w+D=2g-k{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nAlso, recall that acceleration is the derivative of velocity: $a=\\frac{dv}{dt}.$\nSubstituting this into Newton's second law, along with our expression for the resultant force on the rock, we get\n\n$$\n2g-k{v}^{2}=2\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{dv}{dt}=g-\\frac{k}{2}{v}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402141} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe81525-8780-55b2-9e79-394a40fd9a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$50$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402142} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9dd8e3-5764-58b2-9ff2-4b88c32898f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$40$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402143} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5c6a61-12a4-5352-8fcc-e3319bbc18bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402144} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb667f2-6fca-5729-a7fd-9dfe18862602" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$28$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "088dc229-13bb-525f-97f0-615a3387e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$35$ days" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402141} :answer-field/id #uuid "63531a29-5d3e-5f5f-97ce-ef01b7d03202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4fd0915-73cc-575a-b3cc-4a156a96dd49" :question/math-academy-id "q-335542" :question/problem "A wildlife reserve can support a total of $800$ deer.\nAt the beginning of a disease study in the reserve, a parasitic infection has affected $80$ deer. After $10$ days, the number of deer affected by the parasite increases to $300.$ To the nearest day, how long does it take for the infected deer population to reach $600$ animals?\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of deer affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the deer population affected by the parasite, $P(t),$ changes is proportional to the difference between the total deer population and its current population affected. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the total deer population in the reserve, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ deer, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$\nNext, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial deer population affected by the parasite was $80$ animals. So, we apply the initial condition $P(0)=80,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0) & =80 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{0} & =80 \\\\ 800-K & =80 \\\\ K & =720\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-720{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ days, the deer population affected by the parasite was $300$ animals. So, applying the condition $P(10)=300,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(10) & =300 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-10k} & =300 \\\\ 500 & =720{e}^{-10k} \\\\ {e}^{-10k} & =\\frac{500}{720}=\\frac{25}{36} \\\\ -10k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36}) \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when $600$ deer are affected by the parasite. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({t}_{1}) & =600 \\\\ 800-720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =600 \\\\ 200 & =720{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{200}{720}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that $600$ deer will be affected by the parasite after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{18})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{25}{36})}=35.128…≈35\\,\\text{days},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62be6e9-5300-5837-bb21-616f9afabd11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$570$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402148} {:answer/id #uuid "52f73195-6ad4-5063-9185-1d0ce506d0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$560$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402149} {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb7ad78-9e83-5378-a008-fb209e8c0c15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$550$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402151} {:answer/id #uuid "6b3362f7-e0ab-59c9-813b-230e3fd73524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$590$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "487b0c65-385a-5896-a74a-7185c8309375" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$577$ loaves" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402150} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e99660a-207e-56cc-af0c-7810e8c04e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ee0a87d3-d3e9-539a-9074-68a374d85d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335590" :question/problem "A small bakery produces loaves of bread. The owner knows that the bakery can produce a maximum of $600$ loaves a day; however, current production is below this maximum. Since she started monitoring, the bakers produced $480$ loaves and $500$ loaves on days $4$ and $8,$ respectively. How many loaves do they produce on day $40?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the bread production $E(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the bread production $E(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum possible bread production and its current bread production. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(a-E),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum production of the bakery, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=600$ loaves, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dE}{dt}=k(600-E).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dE}{600-E} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dE}{600-E} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|600-E| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $600-E>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $E(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}600-E & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 600-E & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 600-E & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ E & =600-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $4,$ the bread production was $480$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(4)=480,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(4) & =480 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-4k} & =480 \\\\ K{e}^{-4k} & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $8,$ the bread production was $500$ loaves. So, applying the condition $E(8)=500,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E(8) & =500 \\\\ 600-K{e}^{-8k} & =500 \\\\ K{e}^{-8k} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-4k}}{K{e}^{-8k}} & =\\frac{120}{100} \\\\ {e}^{4k} & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\ 4k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{6}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-4\\cdot (1/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} & =120 \\\\ K & =120{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)} \\\\ K & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $E(t):$\n\n$$\nE(t)=600-144{e}^{-(t/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}\n$$\n\nFinally, the number of loaves baked on day $t=40$ is\n\n$$\nE(40)=600-144{e}^{-(40/4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6/5)}≈576.743…≈577\\,\\text{loaves},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b793cda9-e11f-5a48-8053-bb75991c4c42" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Growth Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67f7ba4e-e115-562d-8f0b-e2fb3c50999d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$25\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402155} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402156} {:answer/id #uuid "3c36fd19-5696-5a1e-9dc9-b04f43bf5c32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$12\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402157} {:answer/id #uuid "31324eeb-37b1-5843-9dfa-024be7517c65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$20\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402158} {:answer/id #uuid "11e8a292-e74d-5f79-93dd-d159881ed802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402159}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3fa0d9-3d48-5ce5-bfa8-99fbc7aafb7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$14\\text{th}$ day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402156} :answer-field/id #uuid "450a3a23-c54b-5ed0-90a2-deef088dea63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44cded13-6bc7-52ea-8fcc-d6fe583c3d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-335619" :question/problem "A national park is monitoring the number of people who have downloaded a new park app. The system can support a maximum of $800$ users, but the total is still below this limit. Since the park began tracking downloads, the number of users reached $620$ on day $3$ and $720$ on day $9.$ On what day will the number of users reach $760?$ Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the number of users, $P(t),$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t$ is the time, in days.*" :question/worked-solution "The rate at which the number of app users $P(t)$ changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum population and its current population. Therefore, we have the inhibited growth equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum number of users the system can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=800$ users, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=k(800-P).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{800-P} & =k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dP}{800-P} & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡|800-P| & =kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited growth equation, we have $800-P>0$ for all $t\\ge 0.$ Next, we solve for $P(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}800-P & ={e}^{-kt-C} \\\\ 800-P & ={e}^{-C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ 800-P & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ P & =800-K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{-C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that, on day $3,$ the number of users was $620.$ So, applying the condition $P(3)=620,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3) & =620 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-3k} & =620 \\\\ K{e}^{-3k} & =180.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, on day $9,$ the number of users was $720.$ So, applying the condition $P(9)=720,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(9) & =720 \\\\ 800-K{e}^{-9k} & =720 \\\\ K{e}^{-9k} & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, by dividing the result of the first condition by the result of the second, we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{K{e}^{-3k}}{K{e}^{-9k}} & =\\frac{180}{80} \\\\ {e}^{6k} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 6k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting back into the result of the first condition, we solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{e}^{-3\\cdot (1/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} & =180 \\\\ K & =180{e}^{(1/2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)} \\\\ K & =180\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ K & =180\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $P(t):$\n\n$$\nP(t)=800-270{e}^{-(t/6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9/4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, let $d$  be the day when the app reaches $760$ users. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(d) & =760 \\\\ 800-270{e}^{-kd} & =760 \\\\ 40 & =270{e}^{-kd} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{40}{270} \\\\ {e}^{-kd} & =\\frac{4}{27} \\\\ -kd & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}) \\\\ d & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the number of users will be $760$ on day\n\n$$\nd=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{27})}{\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{4})}=14.1285…≈14,\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest day."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9870a1-f792-53e1-9097-5a43d6035fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402162} {:answer/id #uuid "a89a0be5-8768-5590-83e4-bcbf4ea9fb31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402163} {:answer/id #uuid "b2bb60e5-5192-5666-b667-43311399f89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402164} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba59cf7-16b9-58fc-997b-8944f2b75bc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402165} {:answer/id #uuid "56b2f656-8a92-58eb-ab9c-786f826b15f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c9870a1-f792-53e1-9097-5a43d6035fea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402162} :answer-field/id #uuid "151341a6-4b50-5d9d-ac35-c33852fb4d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cb0dc9ee-4198-5fbc-84d6-225dac676eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335639" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 3x-y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-7y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402167 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c508ee9c-0dee-5d37-9f80-d5e7334160b8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Equilibrium Points of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c43a78-188e-5ac6-8e6c-a2db1d4000a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402169} {:answer/id #uuid "239ae03e-f563-561f-b322-55220d576cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402170} {:answer/id #uuid "3af6e3d8-10db-531f-982e-4b5b3d01053f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402171} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8ac901-6746-5ade-a6a7-36a65ab2324b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402172} {:answer/id #uuid "b232e07f-b421-55de-842f-8f073fd45fa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "239ae03e-f563-561f-b322-55220d576cf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402170} :answer-field/id #uuid "02478602-b3c1-5c9d-81bf-ab4c5f0d8364" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53ed55d4-8280-5930-99c5-60558f8b8c67" :question/math-academy-id "q-335642" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+3y=0 \\\\ 2x+5y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-6y+5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+3\\cdot 0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402174 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b87aeba5-eb67-5dc0-9c25-d9c1cefdb6e2" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Equilibria of the Modified Lotka-Volterra Model: Predator Extinction Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfea3f1f-0f04-5f62-9442-10012cf69aa5" :answer/ty