pe {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402355} {:answer/id #uuid "636fc155-4188-5530-8e60-adb2d62235ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402356} {:answer/id #uuid "41d9b870-5da0-57d1-8c46-b51e8740ed9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca2db5b-65de-5d3d-a904-b52ac412e5a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41d9b870-5da0-57d1-8c46-b51e8740ed9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} :answer-field/id #uuid "819aa321-67db-51f3-9563-c852982e8e87" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402354} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71109050-5463-5ad1-a2e0-992ca3f99166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402360} {:answer/id #uuid "bc720c6e-5092-5a46-980f-2cf9b11293e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71109050-5463-5ad1-a2e0-992ca3f99166" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402360} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6cd00a-0022-5089-9075-29d1a852af1f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402359}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09760520-d150-5b51-9f43-20d7cea8711d" :question/math-academy-id "q-343446" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}4, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ -2, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ -2 & =1-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ -3 & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402363} {:answer/id #uuid "23289d14-87e9-57fb-bbda-cdd305eb9ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-70,\\,z=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402364} {:answer/id #uuid "691a3cdd-d3d5-50e7-a708-646cfa31e0a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-8\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402365} {:answer/id #uuid "823c0a9e-ef4b-5a9d-a5eb-0fe274767e74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=75,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402366} {:answer/id #uuid "ee362d99-5b0e-55c5-83d5-a9a8872163ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402363} :answer-field/id #uuid "0174c191-2a23-5d0f-b8d7-9880c6f7ee41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0076f5-08a5-5d4c-9789-196650ab8295" :question/math-academy-id "q-3435" :question/problem "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3c3466c-dfb0-5b5f-b556-ce57a1d78519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Vector Lies in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0957d54-3a75-5c9c-85ed-c3fc9e27319a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402370} {:answer/id #uuid "a07d906a-9817-56bc-a8bc-f84fb817ea22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402371} {:answer/id #uuid "ffba3c8a-b1b6-5ff8-95f5-61453383f036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402372} {:answer/id #uuid "574e668b-516f-527f-a863-a5d86ad524ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ and ${b}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402373} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7c7a5a-0d78-5275-8321-8300ff247f49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0957d54-3a75-5c9c-85ed-c3fc9e27319a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402370} :answer-field/id #uuid "504c5c81-a875-5c21-b5d2-42ca7fdd3f22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cade4505-b877-5cb5-ae1c-bc1354365d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34361" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}41 \\\\ 82\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 6 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{2}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060278 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f053f6c2-d473-5f4a-9761-a34e3f8fa5eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402376} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c1d7ef-187c-5c23-afd5-1de27fa281b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402377} {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402378} {:answer/id #uuid "79867f36-2c63-53a2-b0d7-906e247536ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402379} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1cfc22-b026-5ac0-aed5-a3a643bfff05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2833382-6b90-5b3c-ae32-77694f3ceaef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402378} :answer-field/id #uuid "88bbe855-4ccb-5da3-a31c-9c9bb8b0b176" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402375}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80f5801f-10e6-5373-bb84-2300dad1ed58" :question/math-academy-id "q-34403" :question/problem "The angle $\\theta =-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ by *adding* integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$\n\n- $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}+2\\cdot (2\\pi )=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ is coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](images/89/891959642b0d055d852329c59be7541179dd5ae0c4182c2bec533261ce3a700d.png)\n\nWe see that $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ lies in quadrant IV. Therefore, $-\\frac{9\\pi }{4}$ also lies in this quadrant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94dc48ed-1523-5b21-aedb-d920ecd4b8a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Indicial Equation of an ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b1137f1-8456-5fcb-b627-0a6bfcd1ccb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402383} {:answer/id #uuid "5e54758b-b04a-5e99-aba8-744f15c11790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda -5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402384} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb98fd7-0760-5ff3-9e0a-7397b57a816d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402385} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcbf29d-5a75-567b-aab9-b022e535042d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402386} {:answer/id #uuid "cd43344f-883e-5e1e-a891-2db669e2c3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda +5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b1137f1-8456-5fcb-b627-0a6bfcd1ccb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7{\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402383} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7e55e1f-40ba-53f0-bfe4-bffbf8e87e07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e617971d-af12-59ac-a1e1-3a955cbb7317" :question/math-academy-id "q-345274" :question/problem "$$\n-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+(x-5)y=0\n$$\n\nFind the indicial equation for a Frobenius series solution about the regular singular point $x=0$ of the differential equation above." :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x-5)y & =(x-5){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } & = \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }. & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n${x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 7{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402388 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e2ef6eb-383e-55db-a5e2-2079a9d1f4f9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the First Solution in the General Solution Using the Method of Frobenius"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14f841b7-2e4c-562e-b920-3f47e5a4c65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402390} {:answer/id #uuid "6b741549-f850-59a1-9e44-07be85fdd39f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{-1}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402391} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3064e5-68b7-55f5-b581-7dd92ce145c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402392} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c6e6a9-137e-5436-abec-4342a46ae334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1/2}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402393} {:answer/id #uuid "738da590-7f4a-5791-85d6-f03977821811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{5/16}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14f841b7-2e4c-562e-b920-3f47e5a4c65b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{1}(x)=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402390} :answer-field/id #uuid "91d07a7f-1c6a-5d61-8b5b-d831b5a92a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1f58e5d-5f27-5c62-b42a-8d5fcfc6f214" :question/math-academy-id "q-345668" :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n16{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+(x-5)x{y}^{′}+(5+2x)y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5=0.$*" :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(16\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{5}{16},1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3c3466c-dfb0-5b5f-b556-ce57a1d78519" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Vector Lies in the Null Space of a Given Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "492978d5-2bec-55d1-bdda-860ad257a369" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402397} {:answer/id #uuid "f6789158-3583-5bab-89c7-6784cdb9397a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{1}$,  ${b}_{2}$, and ${b}_{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402398} {:answer/id #uuid "b68888a5-1eb0-5529-977b-9f744647ef8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402399} {:answer/id #uuid "7354f182-00ec-50bf-9755-be6a57c5b1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402400} {:answer/id #uuid "36c2d878-22ee-54ba-8dc0-b9b9e3b0d0aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{2}$ and ${b}_{3}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b68888a5-1eb0-5529-977b-9f744647ef8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${b}_{1}$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402399} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d0b1afa-4467-52dd-b537-3af075fc6fea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65ef5949-cf70-59d8-adcb-422768352bc5" :question/math-academy-id "q-34984" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and vectors ${b}_{1}$, ${b}_{2}$, ${b}_{3}$ are given below. Which of the vectors lie in $\\text{Null}(A)?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A vector $b$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A)$ if $Ab=0.$ So, we need to compute the product of $A$ and each of the given vectors and check whether it is equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{b}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\\,✓ \\\\ A{b}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-4\\ne 0\\,\\times  \\\\ A{b}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=4\\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only ${b}_{1}$ lies in $\\text{Null}(A).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9039fbb0-0427-5260-9819-2bc08ae0459b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402403} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c93b02-8df2-5914-a21e-f47c872f2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402404} {:answer/id #uuid "ed44325a-1253-529b-97fa-5240ff07dcf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} {:answer/id #uuid "061693b6-a3c5-5e80-8fdd-66ff86213ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402406} {:answer/id #uuid "e63ce889-a7da-51d5-ba30-855cbdeb579a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402407}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c93b02-8df2-5914-a21e-f47c872f2f7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402404} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2749579-8262-5164-918f-9718b15dd845" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb4b9c0-1e83-54e2-ab98-0137b703d096" :question/math-academy-id "q-35363" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic cylinder $9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=18$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $z=1,$ we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $z$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(3)^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402408 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185960eb-bfc4-5b04-9b8e-889e9fa563fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402410} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402411} {:answer/id #uuid "de3fec5a-67b7-579c-a809-55a0c1f4c2ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402412} {:answer/id #uuid "10adf48f-3c4e-533f-ba78-f17c436ead07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402413} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0475d5-57f8-5642-b3fc-c1fb1233e0f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402411} :answer-field/id #uuid "181f4699-3530-5c7f-a1cf-43e09f53ac45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da57a6bc-d0db-59f4-81d7-c54fef1172ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-35379" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124888 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d28eda1a-a90b-57e9-969a-eb36552e413e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Hyperboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19db25b8-9147-5d40-92c3-e1ee457752e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $5\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402416} {:answer/id #uuid "92adda02-c08f-5b9e-9ca7-7b5e715dec45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $10\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402417} {:answer/id #uuid "e2db736f-4ea8-56f7-891b-c509424fd359" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$36$ and $100$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402418} {:answer/id #uuid "9f15a555-a9e1-53df-8497-bf3f68b67562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402419} {:answer/id #uuid "735824d8-08d1-5577-8580-3661f12dcfc7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$18$ and $50$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402420}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f15a555-a9e1-53df-8497-bf3f68b67562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402419} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5953799-7f13-59d3-bf0c-ab698c3e76ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de878af4-60e8-5b69-ad9a-b8c3044d2e69" :question/math-academy-id "q-35410" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50}=1$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the hyperboloid and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the hyperboloid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{18}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{50} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124889 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf44a846-8458-5e2f-bef3-72cf87a6035f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intercepts of a Hyperboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3e1ac47-360d-5d4e-81d1-77973106ffce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402422} {:answer/id #uuid "d9cbb92f-a748-575a-a28c-ccd51fd2a063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402423} {:answer/id #uuid "a0eac9ba-097c-562d-b7e8-c5b232de791f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402424} {:answer/id #uuid "e713d157-180a-577c-a9f9-9ff6ffb97431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402425} {:answer/id #uuid "a32d291b-ec75-560e-9073-c1604179fc76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a32d291b-ec75-560e-9073-c1604179fc76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402426} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cad8bb9-4c82-52b7-8b49-6c5af6fbba05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402421}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49b0818a-b11e-5262-8180-2b40dd5a4460" :question/math-academy-id "q-35429" :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1$, find its $x$-intercepts." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee26a954-2923-5eeb-bf8b-2719bf513f99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402428} {:answer/id #uuid "e26ad3b1-ff38-5495-b616-e54f54399203" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3\\sqrt{3},0,0)$ and $(3\\sqrt{3},0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402429} {:answer/id #uuid "bc68d26e-96f7-5b4d-bdb5-b6fec5a2f084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402430} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fadca9-a9ca-555e-8dea-311a59bf25f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402431} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2d168a-5ddf-5432-87fc-13b24c45ff30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-27,0,0)$ and $(27,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8fadca9-a9ca-555e-8dea-311a59bf25f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402431} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9fe7b8-269f-525e-b0db-d764b7345b0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "863f05c5-808b-57a4-8854-1d3c4552848f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35509" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{12} & =\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74f126da-5f3b-5af8-9aee-d6f0a4c61c49" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Shape of a Level Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ccd00e2-e51e-5254-b333-5c31e7370efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402435} {:answer/id #uuid "e79d828e-64db-52fa-a52e-0190594eb1d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402436} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b008e1-d401-59be-bfbd-56ed2924d87f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402437} {:answer/id #uuid "1746a306-b5b0-58c3-9e66-12d72428be02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402438} {:answer/id #uuid "c67ed439-b900-57c4-ac4d-543a0744030c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e79d828e-64db-52fa-a52e-0190594eb1d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402436} :answer-field/id #uuid "483ba923-28c4-52eb-887b-56bdd19045ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb8b2d04-67d4-51d3-8ebf-71fc804b1a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-35527" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $c=1$ gives a hyperbola\nII. $c=-1$ gives a parabola\nIII. $c=0$ gives the axes $x=0$ and $y=0$" :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=\\pm 1$ then the level curves are $xy=\\pm 1,$ which are hyperbolas.\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curves are $xy=0,$ which consists of the axes $x=0$ and $y=0.$\n\nSo, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402440 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9bdb6bc9-47e2-5330-a1ee-270882f2aced" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Statements Using the Vector Space Axioms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c4f1d1a-384a-5b9f-b52c-026a37aea598" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A3}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402442} {:answer/id #uuid "7be29340-c311-570d-a8a5-d715d90e7b1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A7}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402443} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ffdc61-92b7-525f-ba13-ec7d8e5bece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A7}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402444} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb894df-460c-5d88-98ab-47811b8def6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A2}$, $\\text{A1}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402445} {:answer/id #uuid "27104a21-fa7a-5b79-b863-dfef8c36d3cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A5}$, $\\text{A2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3ffdc61-92b7-525f-ba13-ec7d8e5bece2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{A7}$, $\\text{A3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402444} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7cbdf1-0f6c-51d1-a347-9dc567706a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf85bb24-d080-5608-9b8e-8c02ff8974d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-35559" :question/problem "From top to bottom, fill in the blanks and hence complete the proof of the following statement using the axioms $[\\text{A}1]-[\\text{A}8]$ given below.\n\n*Statement: *\n\n*$0x=0$ for any $x\\in V$* \n\n*Proof:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (0+0)x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{property of number }0\\,] \\\\ 0x+0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0x+(-0x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-0x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x+(0x+(-0x)) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0x+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom __}\\,\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe axioms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{[A1]}:x+y=y+x & \\text{[A5]}:1\\cdot x=x \\\\ \\text{[A2]}:(x+y)+z=x+(y+z) & \\text{[A6]}:\\alpha (\\beta x)=(\\alpha \\beta )x \\\\ \\text{[A3]}:x+0=x & \\text{[A7]}:(\\alpha +\\beta )x=\\alpha x+\\beta x \\\\ \\text{[A4]}:x+(-x)=0 & \\text{[A8]}:\\alpha (x+y)=\\alpha x+\\alpha y\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the vector space axioms, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{given}\\,] \\\\ (0+0)x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{property of number }0\\,] \\\\ 0x+0x & =0x &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A7}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0x+(-0x) &  & [\\,\\text{add }-0x\\text{ to both sides}\\,] \\\\ (0x+0x)+(-0x) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x+(0x+(-0x)) & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A2}\\,] \\\\ 0x+0 & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom A4}\\,] \\\\ 0x & =0 &  & [\\,\\text{axiom }\\text{A3}\\,]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff86a8b-7966-53f7-a7bc-287990fb611d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Traces of Paraboloids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa6485c-6c26-5611-afdf-703d5d2c4fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402449} {:answer/id #uuid "94e8800d-2d94-543f-9790-e394e9982e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two intersecting lines: $y=\\pm 3(x+2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402450} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8e1f20-aacc-5193-b98b-3863146e7855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Two parallel lines: $x=3(z\\pm 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402451} {:answer/id #uuid "0ea80951-99ce-5495-a089-c32574e56f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=27(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402452} {:answer/id #uuid "517d88bc-5b1b-5eeb-90dd-f07f46cd9dc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola: $(y-1)^{2}-{z}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402453}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa6485c-6c26-5611-afdf-703d5d2c4fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola: $z=\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402449} :answer-field/id #uuid "adcc4a54-ddf6-5374-9d97-b2333d8e09c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402448}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69276016-ba7c-50d9-a595-a7d6a823a97c" :question/math-academy-id "q-35577" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{0}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27} \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{27}(y-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives us the equation of the parabola in $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa5aa0d-c161-5543-b5f2-b208ce74003f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402456} {:answer/id #uuid "dd6786f0-4aa2-5b97-bd6a-0f94b037be70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402457} {:answer/id #uuid "ff340c29-e690-5af6-a132-4fdee13b6955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402458} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4b76c4-3a27-545b-a5eb-e8b3696be220" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402459} {:answer/id #uuid "ea902536-cfa7-578c-aba7-32a95d8ea34c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aa5aa0d-c161-5543-b5f2-b208ce74003f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402456} :answer-field/id #uuid "492ab042-d66b-56d3-bdb2-2bf0607f9b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "719356e3-8c2f-52eb-b04e-5bd8a3bf6ec8" :question/math-academy-id "q-35731" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $2$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124911 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d902d5f9-286f-59ed-ad2f-a940f9d64e13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402462} {:answer/id #uuid "59e21a65-e54b-529e-af01-45eee909fed5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402463} {:answer/id #uuid "9e3a2e7f-88af-59d2-b10b-0f6888607047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402464} {:answer/id #uuid "5c862c29-b1f0-5ae3-9332-7d1761b0bb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402465} {:answer/id #uuid "56592c5b-5ea8-5015-81bc-3667c1634320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, $3$rd, and $4$th" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402466}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c862c29-b1f0-5ae3-9332-7d1761b0bb60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402465} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7425100-9aef-51c1-8d0e-fb3c989d9fa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402461}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c93d2ae4-7e7b-5500-9b27-fd939892ffa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-35732" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{4} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2cc6e3-e6bd-532d-8f1f-9b390c501b74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st and $2$nd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402469} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdb0769-e31b-5103-99f8-7c1edc831761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402470} {:answer/id #uuid "a11b1eb4-e2a7-524e-a084-458df3eaa259" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$nd and $3$rd only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402471} {:answer/id #uuid "fc893d60-072c-5dca-bf01-c98cdfe541a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$rd and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402472} {:answer/id #uuid "697bc745-dc44-59f7-92ec-a23865a1d921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "697bc745-dc44-59f7-92ec-a23865a1d921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402473} :answer-field/id #uuid "b29827dc-7c2e-5a8a-9cf8-9120c56fe561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7d0368b2-c48d-55b9-a038-5636deca9c98" :question/math-academy-id "q-35757" :question/problem "What are the pivot columns of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to convert $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 5 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLooking at the row echelon form, we see that $A$ has $3$ pivots. In particular, the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbdd778-7ace-5539-9d6f-cdf01804b237" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Stochastic Matrix Given Some Data"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{: